Order in the logical graph
description: one entry stream, every entry positioned; provenance stays out of the address
- >
  The one ordered container is a graph-level fact: a node has ONE entry stream, and every entry in it
  - keyed or keyless - holds a position. The stream is what the facets describe (keyed and ordinal are
  views of the same entries), what queries enumerate, and what a bare-integer segment addresses.
  [Order is data](*::language:principles:order-is-data) states the principle; this page gives the
  projection rules built on it.
- >
  **Body-anchored members are ordinal.** A directory-backed body whose positional flow names members
  by pointer (`- *file`) reports those members as ordinal: their keys are storage provenance - shown
  as derived, dimmed `&` bookmarks - not authored keys; only the remainder the body never named reports
  as keyed. Fully referenced means pure ordinal - an array; with a remainder, both facets - a mix. The
  rule is per-member, not a prefix: a body that mixes keyed fields with its positional flow scatters
  its bookmarks through source order.
- >
  Display follows: a table of contents names an ordinal member by position (`0`, `1` - the bare index,
  exactly as addressed) rather than by the directory that stores it - a title wins where one exists.
  The label is display; the address is storage.
- >
  **Bookmark-created members come last.** Members that arrive through ordinal bookmarks (`&: path: -`)
  project after all of the container's own in-place entries, ordered lexicographically by member path
  - deterministic and reconstructible from the graph alone. They never affect the container's kind: a
  container's type comes from its owned entries only.
